# Plain English reporting

Default reader: a smart non-engineer. Write status, findings, and
explanations so they can follow without knowing the stack.

## How to talk

- **Lead with what it means, then the mechanism.** "The page was firing
  10 requests when one would do — I made it one" before "consolidated
  the analytics mount into a single aggregate bundle."
- **Translate or drop jargon.** If a term is unavoidable (`TypeError`,
  `deep-freeze`, `memo`), gloss it once in brackets: "the frozen
  response (a locked copy nothing may edit)". Never stack three
  unexplained terms in one sentence.
- **Concrete over abstract.** "The scan runs ~25 checks one after
  another" beats "sequential leg execution"; "the server was mid-
  restart" beats "transient transport failure."
- **Numbers in words a person feels.** "~half a second down to a tenth"
  not "531ms → 109ms p50" — give the raw figure after if it matters.
- **Short sentences.** One idea each. Cut clauses that only hedge.

## Banned vocabulary

- **"Honest" is banned in user-facing text.** Permanently, per the user.
  It reads as disclaimer-padding — "the honest result", "honest limits",
  "honestly" — and a report that needs the word is usually reporting a
  caveat instead of resolving it. Say the thing plainly: "what's left",
  "the real result", "the limits". This covers replies, summaries, and
  report prose. It does not cover quoted machinery text or legacy
  artifact names (those are cited verbatim), and internal gate/script
  strings keep their existing wording.

## What this does NOT change

- Precision stays mandatory: file:line cites, real numbers, unsoftened
  severity, no softening a defect. Plain is not vague — "the request
  died silently" is plain AND precise; "there was an issue" is neither.
- Code, identifiers, config keys, and log strings stay verbatim — they
  are names, not jargon.
- Technical depth stays available on request — if the user asks for the
  mechanism, give it, still in plain English.
- Rule/gate/artifact names stay as named (they are cited by the
  enforcement layer) — but say what they mean the first time.

## The last message after work

Once a task is done, the final reply to the user is a plain-English
summary — and it is the last thing sent, with nothing after it.

- **Issues before the work.** Anything that needs the user's attention
  comes first: risks, things that could not be verified, leftovers or
  follow-ups worth knowing about. Then what was completed.
- **No process chatter.** Internal bookkeeping — gate resolutions,
  artifact edits, scanner cleanups, hook mechanics — stays out unless
  the user asked about it. If internal housekeeping had to happen, it
  gets at most one plain clause inside the summary, never the headline.
- **Their words, not ours.** "The reply bar is shorter and the last
  message stays visible" before any filename or function name.
- **Verification in one line.** What was checked and the real result,
  kept compact — a short list or table at most.
